  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday) \nIssued at 101 AM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nDuring the latter half of the week, models continue to depict an \nupper level low pressure system stalling over the Pacific NW \ncoast. It will draw a rich return flow of Gulf moisture into the \nforecast area Wednesday night and Thursday. Showers and \nthunderstorms will then develop over southern areas Thursday \nafternoon, areawide Thursday night through Friday, then along and\nwest of the central mountain chain Friday night, as a couple of \nshortwave troughs cross NM in succession from the southwest. \n\nThe first shortwave trough will ingest moisture from Tropical \nStorm Vance, which is currently churning off the southwest coast \nof Mexico. It will produce the greatest coverage of showers and \nstorms over the southeast and far eastern plains of NM Thursday \nnight through Friday. GEFS PWATs peak Thursday night into Friday \nand around 180-250 percent of normal, which is quite high. Actual \nPWATs are only forecast to vary from 0.50-1.00\" from northwest to \nsoutheast across the forecast area. The most likely rain amount \nThursday night through Friday along and southeast of a line from \nRuidoso to Tucumcari is forecast to vary around 0.10-1.0\", but NBM\n90TH Percentile QPF suggests a few places will accumulate over \n1.5\" of rain, except over 2\" along and southeast of a line from \nRoswell to Clovis.\n\nThe second shortwave trough will produce scattered to numerous\nshowers and thunderstorms over central and western parts of the\nforecast area Friday afternoon through Friday night. With another\nnice tap of subtropical moisture, rain with this southwesterly \nupslope flow event will favor locations along and west of the \ncentral mountain chain. The most likely rain amount will be under\na quarter inch, except up to a half inch in the southwest \nmountains. NBM 90th Percentile QPF suggests locally heavier \namounts over a half inch, except over 1.5\" in the southwest \nmountains. Temperatures at 700 mb will drop between +1 and +3 \nFriday night, enabling some snow accumulation above 10K FT in \nelevation. \n\nModels are now coming into somewhat better agreement on the track,\ndepth, and timing of a colder storm system with a strong tap of\nPacific moisture that will bring widespread, westerly upslope \nflow, precipitation to the forecast area late Saturday night\nthrough Monday, and potentially Tuesday in the east. Widespread\nwetting precip looks increasingly likely along and west of the\ncentral mountain chain during the latter half of the weekend into\nearly next week with mountain locations potentially accumulating\n1-3\" of rain and liquid equivalent precipitation. Models vary on\nthe track of an upper level low pressure system that will dive\nsoutheastward across CO or NM Monday, but even a northerly track\nacross CO should enable a few to several inches of snow\naccumulation, at least in NM's northern mountains. A more\nsoutherly track over NM would produce widespread mountain snow, \nand potentially some highlands snow as well.",
